ined_indices = final active set (the last list in history). Or we can compute separately.\n\nCheck: Do we use the scores from the current round to rank among active indices? Yes. For forced indices, they are automatically included regardless of score.\n\nEdge cases: If round_targets[-1] is the final number of retained indices. The history should have as many entries as number of rounds? The fixture has two rounds, history length 2? But output schema says history is array of arrays. We'll produce one entry per round.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) in Python. Use standard lib. Return dict with keys 'history' and 'retained_indices'.\n\nMake sure to not mutate case. Copy if needed.\n\nWrite the code as a string inside JSON.\n\nNow, produce the submission envelope: JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value that is a JSON string containing the Python source.\n\nI'll write the code.\n\nLet's think about validation: Check that round_scores, round_targets exist, lengths match, etc. But safety is low.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    round_scores = case['round_scores']\n    round_targets = case['round_targets']\n    forced = case.get('forced', [])  # may be empty\n    # Validate lengths\n    num_rounds = len(round_targets)\n    assert len(round_scores) == num_rounds, \"Mismatch\"\n    N = len(round_scores[0])\n    # Active indices as list sorted\n    active = list(range(N))\n    history = []\n    for r in range(num_rounds):\n        target = round_targets[r]\n        scores = round_scores[r]\n        forced_set = set(forced) & set(active)  # intersection\n        if len(forced_set) > target:\n            raise ValueError(\"Infeasible target: forced exceed target\")\n        # Non-forced actives\n        non_forced = [idx for idx in active if idx not in forced_set]\n        # Sort non_forced by descending score, then take top (target - len(forced_set))\n        non_forced.sort(key=lambda i: scores[i], reverse=True)\n        selected_non_forced = non_forced[:target - len(forced_set)]\n        # Combine forced and selected non-forced, then sort by original index\n        retained = sorted(set(forced_set) | set(selected_non_forced))\n        # Update active for next round\n        active = retained\n        history.append(retained)\n    final_retained = active\n    return {\"history\": history, \"retained_indices\": final_retained}\n\nBut note: forced indices may be not in active if they were removed? But forced indices cannot be removed; we always include them. In our selection, we only include forced that are in active. Initially active includes all, so forced set is all forced. Then subsequent rounds, active contains forced because we always include them. So intersection is fine.\n\nBut there is a subtlety: The forced indices must be included in every round, even if their scores are low. Our method ensures that.\n\nAlso, we need to consider that round_scores might be a list of lists of floats, and we use them for ranking.\n\nNow, ensure we return JSON-serializable (list of list of ints, list of ints).\n\nNow, produce the final output.\n\nI should also note that case may have other fields like _case_nonce, we ignore.\n\nNow, write the JSON string: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nMake sure to escape properly.\n\nI'll write the code with proper indentation.\n\nTesting with the first fixture: round_scores[0] of length 8, round_targets[0]=6, forced=[4]. We select forced index 4, then top 5 scores among non-forced: indices? Scores: [0.87601622, 0.56436, 0.39868851, 0.86799859, 0.95519157 (index4), 0.86151379, 0.14772821, 0.82797916]. Non-forced indices: 0,1,2,3,5,6,7. Sort by score descending: index0 (0.876), index3 (0.868), index5 (0.8615), index7 (0.8279), index1 (0.564), index2 (0.398), index6 (0.147). Top 5: 0,3,5,7,1. Then forced 4, so retained = sorted([4,0,3,5,7,1]) = [0,1,3,4,5,7]. That's 6 indices. Second round: round_scores[1] = [0.66498626,0.36035978,0.30007847,0.01113057,0.18849451 (index4)